workedExamples.solution_text:
Draw a $6\text{ cm}\times4\text{ cm}$ base. Attach a $6\text{ cm}\times1.5\text{ cm}$ rectangle to each long edge and a $4\text{ cm}\times1.5\text{ cm}$ rectangle to each short edge.
One possible net is shown. Dashed lines show folds.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,-1.5)--(6,-1.5)--(6,0)--(7.5,0)--(7.5,4)--(6,4)--(6,5.5)--(0,5.5)--(0,4)--(-1.5,4)--(-1.5,0)--(0,0)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);
\path (3,2) node {$6\times4$};
\path (3,4.75) node {$6\times1.5$};
\path (3,-0.75) node {$6\times1.5$};
\path (-0.75,2) node[rotate=90] {$4\times1.5$};
\path (6.75,2) node[rotate=90] {$4\times1.5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
All dimensions are in centimetres. The four sides fold upwards around the base; there is no top face.

Draw three rectangles in a strip: $3\text{ cm}\times7\text{ cm}$, $4\text{ cm}\times7\text{ cm}$ and $5\text{ cm}\times7\text{ cm}$. Join them along their $7$ cm edges.
Attach one $3$–$4$–$5$ right-angled triangle to each opposite $4$ cm edge of the middle rectangle.
One possible net is shown. Dashed lines show folds.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,-3)--(7,0)--(12,0)--(12,7)--(7,7)--(3,10)--(3,7)--(0,7)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (3,0)--(3,7) (7,0)--(7,7) (3,0)--(7,0) (3,7)--(7,7);
\draw (3,7.4)--(3.4,7.4)--(3.4,7);
\draw (3,-0.4)--(3.4,-0.4)--(3.4,0);
\path (1.5,3.5) node {$3\times7$};
\path (5,3.5) node {$4\times7$};
\path (9.5,3.5) node {$5\times7$};
\path (3,8.5) node[left] {$3$};
\path (5,8.5) node[above right] {$5$};
\path (5,7) node[below] {$4$};
\path (3,-1.5) node[left] {$3$};
\path (5,-1.5) node[below right] {$5$};
\path (5,0) node[above] {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
All dimensions are in centimetres. The rectangle widths match the triangle edges, so the strip folds around the two ends without gaps or overlap.

